  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-qsm-bounded-finite-range-interaction": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nUse the lattice $\\Gamma=\\mathbb Z^d$, $d\\ge1$, the finite spin dimension $q\\ge1$, the local matrix algebras $\\mathcal A_\\Lambda$, and the max-coordinate metric $d_\\infty$ of [[def-qsm-local-spin-algebras]]. For finite nonempty $Z\\subset\\Gamma$, write $\\operatorname{diam}_\\infty Z=\\max_{x,y\\in Z}d_\\infty(x,y)$; for finite nonempty $X,Y\\subset\\Gamma$, write $\\operatorname{dist}_\\infty(X,Y)=\\min_{x\\in X,y\\in Y}d_\\infty(x,y)$.\n\nA **uniformly bounded finite-range interaction** is a family $\\Phi(Z)\\in\\mathcal A_Z$, indexed by finite nonempty $Z\\subset\\Gamma$, such that $\\Phi(Z)^*=\\Phi(Z)$ and there are an integer $M\\ge1$, a finite $R\\ge0$, and $\\kappa<\\infty$ satisfying\n\n$$\\Phi(Z)=0\\quad\\text{if }|Z|>M\\text{ or }\\operatorname{diam}_\\infty Z>R,\\qquad\\kappa:=\\sup_{x\\in\\Gamma}\\sum_{Z\\ni x}\\|\\Phi(Z)\\|<\\infty.$$\n\nThe norm in this sum is the operator norm in the local matrix algebra $\\mathcal A_Z$. If a model lists several terms with the same support, first add them in that local algebra; the resulting single operator is $\\Phi(Z)$, and $\\kappa$ uses its norm. Thus no multiplicity is implicit.\n\nFor a finite region $\\Lambda\\subset\\Gamma$, define the **free-boundary finite-volume Hamiltonian** by summing only terms whose complete support lies inside $\\Lambda$; terms meeting $\\Lambda$ but extending outside it are omitted:\n\n$$H_\\Lambda:=\\sum_{\\varnothing\\ne Z\\subseteq\\Lambda}\\Phi(Z)\\in\\mathcal A_\\Lambda.$$\n\nIt is a finite sum of self-adjoint matrices. For a fixed scale $\\hbar>0$ and any finite $\\Omega\\supseteq\\Lambda$, embed $H_\\Lambda$ in $\\mathcal A_\\Omega$ by the canonical factor inclusion and define\n\n$$\\alpha_t^\\Lambda(A):=e^{itH_\\Lambda/\\hbar}Ae^{-itH_\\Lambda/\\hbar}\\qquad(A\\in\\mathcal A_\\Omega,\\ t\\in\\mathbb R).$$\n\nThese actions are compatible as $\\Omega$ increases, because the extra tensor factors carry the identity. On $\\mathcal A_\\Lambda$ this is the ordinary finite matrix Heisenberg dynamics.\n\nIn physical SI bookkeeping, when $\\Phi(Z)$ is interpreted as an energy, $\\Phi(Z)$, $\\kappa$, and $H_\\Lambda$ have units J, $t$ has units s, and $\\hbar$ is the adopted reduced Planck constant with units J s. The mathematics below uses only $\\hbar>0$ and asserts no measured parameter value."
    ],
    "lem-qsm-finite-range-commutator-path-bound": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Phi$ be an interaction of [[def-qsm-bounded-finite-range-interaction]] with parameters $M,R,\\kappa,\\hbar$. Let $\\mu>0$, let $\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb Z^d$ be finite, let $X\\subseteq\\Lambda$ be finite and nonempty, and let $Y\\subset\\mathbb Z^d$ be any finite nonempty set. Take $A\\in\\mathcal A_X$ and $B\\in\\mathcal A_Y$, with both embedded in the common finite algebra $\\mathcal A_{\\Lambda\\cup Y}$. Then for every $t\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$\\bigl\\|[\\alpha_t^\\Lambda(A),B]\\bigr\\|\\le 2\\|A\\|\\,\\|B\\|\\,|X|\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{2\\kappa M e^{\\mu R}}{\\hbar}|t|-\\mu\\operatorname{dist}_\\infty(X,Y)\\right).$$\n\nHere the finite-volume dynamics is extended by the identity on sites outside $\\Lambda$, as in [[def-qsm-bounded-finite-range-interaction]]. The parameter $\\mu$ is measured per lattice step in the dimensionless max-coordinate metric. The bound is uniform over finite volumes containing $X$; it is an operator-norm estimate for the stipulated spin model, not a relativistic light cone."
    ],
    "lem-qsm-boundary-dynamics-cauchy": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Phi$ be an interaction of [[def-qsm-bounded-finite-range-interaction]], fix $\\mu>0$, and let $X\\subseteq\\Lambda\\subseteq\\Lambda'$ be finite regions with $X\\ne\\varnothing$. For every $A\\in\\mathcal A_X$ and $t\\in\\mathbb R$,\n\n$$\\|\\alpha_t^{\\Lambda'}(A)-\\alpha_t^\\Lambda(A)\\|\\le\\frac{2\\kappa |X|\\|A\\||t|}{\\hbar}e^{(2\\kappa M e^{\\mu R}/\\hbar)|t|+\\mu R}\\sum_{y\\notin\\Lambda}e^{-\\mu\\operatorname{dist}_\\infty(X,\\{y\\})}.$$\n\nThe infinite sum is finite. It tends to zero as $\\Lambda$ exhausts $\\mathbb Z^d$, so the estimate gives compact-time norm Cauchy convergence of free-boundary finite-volume dynamics on each local observable."
    ],
    "thm-qsm-finite-range-spin-dynamics": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice and the finite-matrix C*-identity condition [A1] below. Let $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{loc}}=\\bigcup_{\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb Z^d,\\ |\\Lambda|<\\infty}\\mathcal A_\\Lambda$ and let $\\mathcal A$ be its norm completion as in [[def-qsm-quasilocal-spin-algebra]]. These assumptions give the conditional unital C*-algebra structure supplied by [[lem-qsm-quasilocal-completion]]. Let $\\Phi$ be a uniformly bounded finite-range interaction of [[def-qsm-bounded-finite-range-interaction]].\n\nFor any increasing finite exhaustion $\\Lambda_n\\uparrow\\mathbb Z^d$, the free-boundary finite-volume maps $\\alpha_t^{\\Lambda_n}$ converge in norm on every local observable, uniformly for $t$ in compact intervals. Their limit is independent of the exhaustion and extends uniquely to a strongly continuous one-parameter group $\\alpha_t$ of $*$-automorphisms of $\\mathcal A$. Strong continuity means $t\\mapsto\\alpha_t(A)$ is norm-continuous for every fixed $A\\in\\mathcal A$. This result does not assert that an infinite-volume Hamiltonian is an element of $\\mathcal A$ or a bounded operator on a preselected infinite tensor-product Hilbert space."
    ],
    "lem-qsm-local-generator-domain": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nUnder the hypotheses of [[thm-qsm-finite-range-spin-dynamics]], including its Countable Choice and finite-matrix C*-identity conditions, let $\\alpha_t$ be the strongly continuous automorphism group. Define its generator by\n\n$$D(\\delta)=\\left\\{A\\in\\mathcal A:\\lim_{t\\to0}\\frac{\\alpha_t(A)-A}{t}\\text{ exists in norm}\\right\\},\\qquad\\delta(A)=\\lim_{t\\to0}\\frac{\\alpha_t(A)-A}{t}.$$\n\nThen $\\delta$ is closed. For every local $A\\in\\mathcal A_X$,\n\n$$A\\in D(\\delta),\\qquad\\delta(A)=\\frac{i}{\\hbar}\\sum_{Z:\\,Z\\cap X\\ne\\varnothing}[\\Phi(Z),A].$$\n\nThe sum is finite by the finite-range and finite-size hypotheses; its value is independent of the chosen localization region $X$."
    ],
    "ex-qsm-finite-range-nearest-neighbor-ising": [
      null
    ]
  }
